  "summary": "Near-complete assemblies from long reads now expose centromere, telomere and immune-region variation in oyster, pig and malaria parasite genomes.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is assembly-first genomics: long-read and telomere-to-telomere-grade de novo assemblies that resolve repetitive and structurally complex regions, then used for trait mapping. The oyster chromosome-level assembly links an expanded chr1 immune region and RNF213 to oyster size through GWAS and k-mer analysis. The pig pan-genome combines three T2T and sixteen high-quality assemblies to resolve centromere evolution, structural variation and genotype-phenotype mapping, alongside a pan-transcriptome and pan-metabolome. The Malian Plasmodium falciparum study applies optimized PacBio HiFi assembly to eight cloned field isolates to recover near-complete genomes. GapCNV is a methods item rather than an assembly study, addressing copy-number detection in low-input DNA sequencing. What is new is the extension of near-complete assembly to a livestock pan-genome, a parasite field-isolate set and an invertebrate genome.",
